Means, fluctuations, correlations and sampling (@fd-turbulent-statistics: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @fluid-dynamics, #source "../sources/Turbulence-McDonough.lingua#r_2TBK3JC1KJ1CMNABRW3Y2AFKTQ", #source "../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA", #source "../sources/Johns-Hopkins-Turbulence-Databases.lingua#r_1D25T86CC1CHT4BX098WQXBQPE", #source "../sources/Experimental-Methods-in-Fluids-Mondal.lingua#r_4XFYEGQVB1EV4QF9BP17ME04Y4", #fluid-dynamics "Reynolds-averaging-stress-transport-and-the-closure-problem.lingua#r_2WR3KYF31R7RJSN20P6PKS0PM0") { r_28QSWBMS4143WE5A6NHQQJXZEY
**Read:** [Introductory Lectures on Turbulence — James M. McDonough](../sources/Turbulence-McDonough.lingua#r_2TBK3JC1KJ1CMNABRW3Y2AFKTQ), statistical descriptions of turbulence; [Fluid Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA), chapter 6 averages and correlations; [Johns Hopkins Turbulence Databases — numerical flow evidence](../sources/Johns-Hopkins-Turbulence-Databases.lingua#r_1D25T86CC1CHT4BX098WQXBQPE), selected dataset metadata and published statistical definitions; [Experimental Methods in Fluid Mechanics — Pranab K. Mondal](../sources/Experimental-Methods-in-Fluids-Mondal.lingua#r_4XFYEGQVB1EV4QF9BP17ME04Y4), response and statistical measurement sections.

Choose an averaging operation before writing u=U+u′. Ensemble, time, phase and spatial averages answer different questions and require different assumptions to interchange. A time record is not automatically stationary, and closely spaced samples are not independent. Report the measured component, location, bandwidth, acquisition duration and conditioning on events.

Define variance, covariance, correlation and spectra with their normalizations. Relate the velocity covariance tensor to turbulent kinetic energy k=⟨u′·u′⟩/2. Check tensor symmetry and nonnegative variance in any direction. Spatial or temporal filtering changes the resolved variance; unresolved motion and instrumental noise cannot be separated merely by drawing a spectrum.

Estimate a mean and stress using successively longer record segments, inspect drift and integral correlation times, and state the uncertainty method. For a numerical database, preserve forcing, grid, interpolation and averaging choices. Distinguish a statistical uncertainty due to finite sampling from a biased physical or numerical model.

**Continue:** [Reynolds averaging, stress transport and the closure problem](Reynolds-averaging-stress-transport-and-the-closure-problem.lingua#r_2WR3KYF31R7RJSN20P6PKS0PM0).

